   288	
   289	### New canon minted (author's list)
   290	- **Where Thornwater's ford lies (flag):** the upper of the two shallow crossings of the valley's water that the party made on day 43; below the mouth of the stretch; between two and three hours' walk below the gate. The pack that paced the cart on the stretch is the ford's pack.
   291	- **The ford's ground (flag):** a river some fifty yards wide over a single ledge of grey rock, **the sill**, ten feet broad, with two wheel-grooves and green slime; shin-deep fast water over it; a slow glide above; below, a **lip**, and pools that fall away over a man's head. A gravel **bar** in mid-river, thirty yards by eight, lying with the stream, its blunt head at the sill and its tail running down between the two pools to a point. A near (Thornwater-side) and a far channel, twenty yards each; a near and a far beach; a cut bank a man's height above the near beach.
   292	- **The lip stones (flag):** a row of white stones, a yard apart, which the hold sets along the sill's downstream lip each year "when the water drops", so that a wheel can see where the rock stops. Five yards of the row were unset on day 52; the row is whole by day 58.
   293	- **The third (flag):** two men of the hold went down at noon on the 49th to set the last stones. One, a Force, lifted with his Path under water to free the other's leg and was let alone; he stood up on the dry bar at dusk and was taken. The other got his hand between a hound's jaws and his own throat, was left when the Force shouted, and walked home in the dark. Neither is named.
   294	- **What the hold knows:** "Dusk. Never noon."; the bowman has had the gate-walk thirty years, has seen one come up the grass four evenings in five, and **set the white stone himself in his first year, where they stop**. He cannot say why there. The 110 yards stay unaccounted for.
   295	- **The finding (flag), as dictated by Karis and written in Cael's log:** the stillhound hunts Path discharge; it hears and sees a little, and uses both only to keep its place and to try a quiet body with its teeth; water masks it at both ends (a wading hound goes to where the declaration was when it left dry ground; a Path held under water for an hour and a quarter drew nothing at eleven feet); there is no leader, only whichever moves first; they learn. Not accounted for: 110 yards; dusk.
   296	- **"Discharge"** is Karis's lecturer's word for what leaves a person when a Path is used; assessors call it presence and mark it; a station reads it off a plate.
   297	- **A fragment answering is felt by Cael as something going out**, like the others' discharge. Whether a hound can tell it from a Path is untested; he holds on the supposition that it cannot. The one burst drew three.
   298	- **Seven hounds at the ford:** six that lie up in the far bank's grass and come down by three paths; a seventh, older, white-muzzled, with a torn left ear, that lies on the bar's tail. They drink one at a time and will not wet a foot.
   299	- **Seln's Path under water (flag):** on day 53 he lifts his own Shadow for the first time since the crossroads fire, to the chin in the near pool, and lets it go before he rises. His knife: narrow, dull, a hand long. "The cut" is his trade's word for the man placed where the subject will run.
   300	- **The hold's arrangements:** the bowman goes down as the hirer's witness and stands on the cut bank; the carter waits with the cart at the last bend; Seln's case stays in the loft between the second box and the third for that one evening.
   301	- **Injuries, as fixed on the page:** Brom, left forearm, two furrows from wrist to mid-arm, to the bone, fourteen stitches; opened again at the wrist on day 59 (a hand put out to steady the cart in a wet rut), seen at the fire that night; stitches out on day 64; closed clean and re-wrapped on day 66. Lira, right ankle, the ligaments, bound flat to a barrel-stave; a stick from the bowman on day 56, left against the inn wall on day 64. Karis, two cuts across each palm; bound fists, then strips; a pencil on day 68. Cael, left calf, in at the outside and out at the inside; eleven strides, then twenty (day 62), then "a leg on level ground" and nine uphill (day 69).
   302	- **Thornwater:** a long house with a hearth across one end and a trestle; fourteen people at supper; a sack-mending trestle in the barn; the hold charges one mark for linen and thread. East carts fetch grain on day 58 by the east gate.
   303	- **The carter's map (flag):** east of Thornwater, two days of ticks out, the double line leaves its straight, goes round a patch of bare hide and comes back to its line, with nothing drawn inside the bend. Karis calls it "a quiet place on the hide"; that word draws the carter's wife's three sentences.
   304	- **A flint fire is not a declaration (flag):** on the 59th, above the wash, the crew lights its first road fire since the fifth day, on the finding's first sentence. The hold itself stands unchanged. The Thornwater carter sits at it and says that he will not do it alone.
   305	- **Lowmarch:** undirected eastbound mail stops at Pike's plank; he gives out each piece twice and reads letters at dusk to people who have no letters; a spike under the plank holds finished slips; he rewrites the whole wall on fresh sized sailcloth when the order changes; a crew entered as *the shingle pair* is thirteenth from day 60.
   306	- **Teague:** back down the cold road on the 57th; read the struck slip twice and the five's line; took two holds' slips from the north end of the plank and went up the north water at next light. Not seen.
   307	- **The well (flag):** at the south road's fork, half a day out on level ground; a dry-stone curb, a windlass; paid from the standing-money. It went sour at the thaw; a carter's boy was drawn up asleep. Foul air lay fifteen feet deep on the water at thirty feet. Pike's line: *The well at the south fork. Air cleared, shaft drawn, six stones set. Four marks.*
   308	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the hold's boy on the leaning mule; the man with the bound arm; the clouded-eyed east carter, his wife, and the small boy on her foot-board; the shingle pair; the southbound carter whose son went down the well.
