     1	# Chapter 5 — Route
     2	
     3	The slip was in a hold's hand, and the hold was one he had never heard of.
     4	
     5	He read it at the plank on the seventieth morning past the Line, in the order the board taught, which was every slip once and then the ones that paid twice: *Oxhollow ford. High traverse to Thornwater wanted scouted before the autumn carts. Shale bench above the gorge; two days under the void road; not walked three seasons, since the slide. Map wanted, and a word on whether it holds a cart. Seventy marks on the map, paid at Oxhollow.*
     6	
     7	Oxhollow was a ford, north, a week by the void road; that was the whole of what the plank knew about it.
     8	
     9	Thirteen slips. Two of them, the good two, wore Pike's HOLD and Teague's name across the top in the same pencil; the crew had been in off the autumn road and out again with the hold slips while the five of them were at the well on the south road. That left eleven: one hold's, four escorts at the carters' price, and fences and wells and a roof that wanted thatching. He sorted them by what each would cost the five of them, and the sorting put Oxhollow's slip at the top by itself before he had finished reading it the second time.
    10	
    11	He counted why, because he did not trust a thing that sorted itself.
    12	
    13	It paid. Seventy marks was the ford's price and three-quarters again, for a walk. Holds priced what a thing was worth to the hold, and a route two days under the void road was worth a great deal to a ford that sent carts south in autumn.
    14	
    15	Teague's crew was gone on the hold contracts, a week out and a week back, and Oxhollow's slip had come in on a carter behind them and sat four days on the plank with nobody's finger on it. Pike had said, to the page, when Cael asked why: "Pays on the map, at Oxhollow. That's a week north to get paid for a week's walk, and the crews that could take it are the crews with the pick. Nobody's been on the board long enough to want it."
    16	
    17	And the third reason, which he counted last because it was his: it was ground. Not teeth. A scouting run was a thing walked and measured and written down and handed to a man who would trust a cart to the writing, and he had done that every day of his life since he was fourteen, in a notebook, for nothing, at the edges of rooms where the work being paid for was somebody else's. He had never once been paid to catalogue. He read the slip a third time to be sure. It was what it said.
    18	
    19	"That one," he said.
    20	
    21	Seln, beside him, had already put a finger on it and moved it a quarter-inch on the plank.
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	Pike entered it as a price gets entered, flat, with the five names under it, and said the things he said.
    26	
    27	"Three seasons nobody's walked it. Slide came down on the Thornwater end and the carts went round by the void road and stopped talking about the bench. Oxhollow doesn't want a crew that comes back and says it's fine." The pen did not stop. "They want a map they can put a cart on. Width at every narrow. Where it holds and where it doesn't. Where you'd camp." A pause, at the end of a line. "Take a cart. Smith has one, six marks against it coming back. You want to tell a ford it holds a cart, you'd better have had one on it."
    28	
    29	He said one more thing, to Seln and not to the rest of them, when Seln stopped at the plank's end on the way out: "Ask me about the shale-backs before you go." Seln asked, there, with his back to the room, and Cael did not go to hear the answer, because a man who asked a thing quietly at the end of a plank had asked it that way on purpose.
    30	
    31	The cart was the smith's second-best, two wheels and a bed and a pair of shafts with a chest-strap between them. Brom stood in the shafts and put the strap across his chest and leaned on it once, and it moved, and he nodded, and that was the cart's whole trial. The bound arm went inside the right shaft against his ribs. The good hand took the left.
    32	
    33	The boxes went because there was nowhere to leave them. Lowmarch had no wall and a population that turned over with the season, and three boxes in a meadow belonged to whoever walked past them last. Karis roped them onto the cart's bed herself, the front box over the axle and the other two behind it, and put her hand flat on the middle lid when she was done, as if to see whether it was warm.
    34	
    35	Inventory, taken on the smith's yard on the seventy-first morning.
    36	
    37	The calf: closed, mostly. He had stopped counting on the flat, because the count had gone past twenty and a number that large was not a limp, it was a leg. Uphill it still said nine. He had not told anyone the uphill number, and there was a mountain in the slip.
    38	
    39	Brom's forearm: bound wrist to elbow still, but the wrist had closed at last, the cloth coming off clean on the sixty-sixth day, and he had looked at the closed place with the lock face and wrapped it again as if it had not. The hand was allowed to hold. It was not allowed to pull. The chest-strap pulled.
