   207	*The traverse holds. So far. The map's good — Karis's, not mine; mine's the check. The herd's above the eighth narrowing tonight, thirty, grazing along. Karis says they go off declarations, as a unit, away, and on a slope away is down; which means the loudest thing on this mountain is us, and we've been quiet four days. Not one of us has had to try.*
   208	
   209	*Nothing's drifted in four days. I'm writing that as a fact and not as a meaning. First honest job I've ever had that didn't have a fight at the end of it. I'm writing that before it's finished, which I don't do. Note that I did.*
   210	
   211	---
   212	
   213	The fifth day showed them the ledge.
   214	
   215	They saw it from the ninth narrowing in the last of the light, because the bench had bent round a shoulder of the mountain and given them the next stretch whole: the shelf running on along the face, a cart wide, then less than a cart, then — a long way ahead, where the mountain bulged out over the gorge — pinching down to a lip that was, at that distance, a line. A ledge. Below it, the gorge, wider there than anywhere they had seen it. Above it, the upper shelf, coming round the same shoulder with the herd on it, thirty backs on the last green, above them and — he counted the narrowings back — behind them now, for the first time, by half a day's grazing. Beyond the ledge, the bench opened again and went on toward Oxhollow.
   216	
   217	Karis stood with the cord in her hand and did not run it across anything. "I can't measure that from here. Morning."
   218	
   219	"Here," Seln agreed, and it was, of course, an overhang.
   220	
   221	Two-thirds. He did the count on the tail-board while the fire was lit: nine narrowings in the book with their widths; Pike's seven or eight days for a cart, and five of them walked. A map a hold could put a cart on, for two-thirds of a mountain. The last third began with a line.
   222	
   223	Lira sat down by the fire before anyone and took off the boot.
   224	
