   226	
   227	She tied it off and looked, not at any of them, at the shoulder of the mountain with the ledge on the far side of it.
   228	
   229	"I'll run it at first light," she said. "See if it holds a cart."
   230	
   231	Brom, from the strap, which he had not taken off: "It'll hold you."
   232	
   233	"It'll hold me, and I'll know what it holds." She put the boot on over the tape and stood on it, once, to feel where it bent. "That's what I'm for up here. That's the job. First light, before the sun's on it."
   234	
   235	Nobody argued. It had been the plan at every narrowing since the fan, and the ledge was a narrowing with a name.
   236	
   237	Seln was looking up the mountain at the herd. Brom took the strap off at last and sat with the bound arm inside the good one. The gorge gave the fire's small noises back to them, late, from across the gap.
   238	
   239	He took the log out.
   240	
   241	---
   242	
   243	*Seventy-seventh day. Fifth camp, at the ninth narrowing. The ledge is in sight; Lira runs it at first light.*
   244	
   245	*The traverse holds. The map's good. Karis says the herd moves off declarations, which means the loudest thing on this mountain is us, and we've been quiet for five days. One more day. First honest job I've ever had that didn't have a fight at the end of it. Sleep.*

