    99	---
   100	
   101	Lira came in last.
   102	
   103	He had lost her at the ledge's root when he ran; everyone had. Now the bench was a different bench, plated over new to the edge, and she was forty paces off where she had landed, coming on her hands.
   104	
   105	Seln went out to her. Cael saw him go and could not have.
   106	
   107	She had gone for the fin when they went for it, on the ankle that had gone at the ford and gone again when she landed; he pieced that afterward from the marks in the shale. What had come down on her had not been an animal. It had been the bench: a plate going out under her foot as the second half came down and the whole shelf shifted, the leg going into the gap it left, and the plate above coming down on the leg with the mountain behind it. The plate was still there. Seln lifted it off. Under it the leg below the knee was the wrong shape. Not bent; the wrong shape, a line with an angle in it where there was not supposed to be an angle. The foot had no boot. The ledge had taken the boot. A strip of tape was still round the ankle above the bare foot, tied off at the shin.
