   153	That night the fire was small, lit with a flint, because the woman who lit fires was asleep against a box. Brom did the food, what had been in the packs and not the cart: a day and a half at the ford's rate for five, and Brom made it three at a rate he did not explain. Cael lay on his right side in the fin's lee with the box for a pillow and counted the breaths that stopped at the place, and did not take the log out, because he could not have held it and did not want to see the hand.
   154	
   155	---
   156	
   157	The next day was the one the mountain kept. Seln went at first light with the pack on and came back before the sun was over the gorge: the lip held a walker, and below the lip the fan the herd had taken down was still going, in slides, one every hundred breaths, and a man on it was a stone on it. "Tomorrow," he said, "or it's two of us." The day was rationing and the leg. Karis woke and could not light a lamp and did not try again. Lira's leg went hot under the plates by noon, and the tape came off and went back on wetted, and she said nothing about it, which was how Cael knew. He counted breaths, and the slides on the fan, and the gorge, and did not take the log out.
   158	
   159	---
   160	
   161	The third morning came up grey, and the sound of the gorge came up with it.
   162	
