   226	
   227	"Ember," she said. "And nothing. You're empty. That's not a hurt; I can't put back what you spent, and I wouldn't if I could. Eat. Sleep. It fills." She looked at the black line the lattice had left across the face above the fin, thirty paces of scorched shale, and then at Karis, and there was no awe in it at all. "That's yours?"
   228	
   229	"That's mine."
   230	
   231	"Hm." The whole of the verdict. She stood.
   232	
   233	He had the log out by then. He had got it out of the satchel with the right hand while she was at Brom's arm, and found that he could hold the pencil if he did not breathe past the place. He wrote one line. It was not his hand. It was a hand that hurt, and it leaned, and it was legible.
   234	
   235	*Eightieth day. Lira's leg is straight. The boxes are ours. The cart is in the gorge. A healer came up the road because a carter said we were late.*
   236	
   237	He closed it. She was standing over him.
   238	
   239	---
   240	
   241	"You," she said. "Ribs. I saw you breathe."
   242	
   243	"Three. Left side. Low."
   244	
   245	"I'll be the one who says that." She knelt in the shale at his side and did not ask him to move, and put both hands on him, one flat on the ribs where the shoulder had taken him, one on his back behind it, and went still.
   246	
   247	Seconds. He counted them. He watched her face because it was in front of him: the face of a woman listening for a thing she had heard a thousand times, as a fighter listens for a step behind him.
   248	
   249	She stopped.
   250	
   251	Not as she had stopped at the leg or the arm, at the end of the seconds, with the finding ready. She stopped in the middle of them. Her hands came off him. She looked at them, both, palm and back, as a man looks at a tool that has done a thing it has never done. Then she put them back, exactly where they had been, and went still again, and held it longer, and her face did a thing.
   252	
   253	He had watched that thing on faces for four years. At fourteen, for eleven seconds, from the inside. On assessors' faces at Ardenmere and at Greyvane, and on a panel in the academy above Ostrand, from the outside, over a page with his name on it and a bracket after it. It was the face of an instrument that has been put to a thing and has returned nothing, not a wrong number, no number, and is running itself again to be sure. He had never seen it on a healer. Healers did not read the page. Healers read the leg.
   254	
   255	She took her hands off him and did not look at them this time. She looked at him.
   256	
   257	"Your ribs are broken," she said. "I can feel that they're broken. I can't feel *you*."
   258	
   259	Nobody moved. Lira's eyes had opened. Karis, against the box, did not move anything but her eyes.
   260	
   261	"Whatever you are, I can't read it." It was said as the leg had been said: a list, exact, at the pace of a woman who did not hurry a finding because the finding was bad. "I've never said that to anyone. Tape them. They'll heal on their own." A breath. "They always have, haven't they?"
   262	
   263	"Yes," he said.
   264	
   265	"That's not an answer," Oryn said. "That's a *finding*."

