     1	# Chapter 7 — The Healer's Route
     2	
     3	She taped his ribs on the eighty-first morning, before the sun was on the bench, and told him what it was while she did it.
     4	
     5	"This is cloth," Oryn said. "Anybody can do cloth. Breathe out. All of it." She took the linen round him under the arm and over the place three times, pulling at each turn until the breath he had left stopped where she wanted it and not where the shale-back had decided. "There. Now it stops at the tape. That's the whole of what tape does. It doesn't mend anything. It tells the ribs where the edge is so they stop looking for it." She tied it off at the front, flat, with the knot where a strap would not lie. "You'll hate it by noon. Leave it."
     6	
     7	He breathed in and the breath stopped a finger's width sooner than it had for three days, at a place he could feel from the outside, and it was better. He did not say so. She had not asked.
     8	
     9	Then the lip.
    10	
    11	Seln went first, one hand on the mountain, and stood at the far end where the bench opened and the mule was, and waited. Lira went second. The bone was whole, Oryn had said, and new, and today it took weight; she stood up off the shale on it with her face doing nothing and the bare foot under the taped ankle going grey in the cold, and she walked the lip as she had walked the ledge, feet first, reading it, slower, with a hand on the rock the whole way. Oryn went behind her. Not close. A stride and a half — near enough to take a hand, far enough not to be taken by one; and Cael, who had catalogued where people stood since he was fourteen, catalogued it. She had put herself where a fall would come. Not in front, where Seln was, and not at the back, where Brom would be. Where the hurt would arrive, if it arrived.
    12	
    13	The boxes went next, one at a time, on backs. Brom put the strap of the first over his shoulders and Oryn looked at the mended arm inside his sleeve and said "Carrying," and Brom said "Not pulling," and she said "Hm," and it stood. Seln came back for the second. The third went on the mule.
    14	
    15	Cael went last, with Seln behind him, because Seln had come back a third time without being asked. Thirty paces of the mountain's edge with nothing under the outer foot; a hand on the rock and the next foot, and the breath stopping at the tape, and the calf saying what it had said since the shale. He counted the paces because there was nothing else to count, and got thirty-one, and stepped off onto the wide bench, and the mountain was behind them.
    16	
    17	The mule stood where it had stood for two days, tied to nothing. It looked at six people and three boxes as a mule looks at anything, and put its head down.
    18	
    19	---
    20	
    21	Four days down.
    22	
    23	He wrote that at the first halt, because he had the log and the satchel and nothing else — the first time since the Line that he had come off a job carrying nothing but paper — and the sentence was the shape of the thing he was in. Four days: the bench going down and widening and losing its edge until it was a slope and then a hillside; the shale giving way to turf in patches, and then to turf with shale in it; the gorge opening below into a valley with Oxhollow's river at the bottom of it. Lira on the mule, the bare foot in a sock of Oryn's linen, saying nothing about being on a mule. Brom under a box. Seln under a box, ahead, and then not ahead, and then ahead again. Karis with the packs and the map, drawing the bench down as she had drawn it up. And the healer.
    24	
    25	He catalogued her walk on the first day, because it was the first thing she did in front of him that was not her hands.
    26	
    27	She walked like a carter: the pace set once at the start of a road and not changed for a slope or a narrow or anything short of an animal in the road; the feet going down whole and staying down; the weight never once ahead of them. Lira moved as if the ground were a thing she could leave. Oryn moved as if it were a thing she had an arrangement with. She was never fast. In four days he did not see her hurry, and he was watching for it — at the mule's stumbles, at the place on the second day where the ground went out from under Brom's box — and she came to each at the same walk, and was there when she was needed and not before. Item. She walked on the down-slope side of the mule, where a rider would come off. Item. She looked at the four of them who could walk as Seln looked at a road, every halt, once, all together, and then at whichever one had changed. Item. She had a heel gone raw in her boot from the mountain, and at the first halt she took the boot off and washed it and taped it with the same grey cloth she had put round his ribs, and put the boot back on, and walked. She did not put her hands on it and go still. She could not do that to herself. She had said so on the bench, and here it was, in a boot.
    28	
    29	Lira asked her about the ankle at the second day's noon.
    30	
    31	They had stopped where the slope flattened into turf, and Lira sat against a stone with the leg out, and said it as she said everything, as a fact for checking: "You could do the ankle."
    32	
    33	"I could."
    34	
    35	"But."
    36	
    37	"Three." Oryn had her skin out and was drinking, not much. "Three a day, if I'm fed and I've slept, and that's a good day; two's more like it on a road. That's what I've got, all told, for everybody I put my hands on between here and the next place I sleep." She capped the skin. "I spent three on your mountain. A leg, a wrist, and a leg's worth of walking up. Oxhollow was owed a visit six days ago and I was on a bench instead. Somebody at Oxhollow's got worse in six days. I don't know who yet. There's always someone." She looked at the ankle, once. "That's a tape and a season. It'll set. Somebody at the ford's a leg, or a chest, or a child. Your ankle's not going to be why they didn't get me."
    38	
    39	Lira looked at the ankle, and then at the healer, and said, "Right." She did not ask again. He had known her four years and could count on a hand the things she had asked for twice.
    40	
    41	He put the arithmetic on the shelf beside Vell's. It was the same shape. A finite thing, kept in a head, spent by a rule the keeper had not made and would not break, and the rule was not who deserved it. It was who was next on the road.
    42	
    43	At the same halt Oryn came round the stone to him and said "Calf," and he gave it to her: Seln's binding, four days old and stiff with the mountain, and the wound under it, the ford's two mouths opened along the shale and closed again, mostly, as things closed on him. She looked at it as a carter looks at a wheel. She washed it from her skin and bound it fresh in her cloth, tight, and did not put her hands on it and go still.
    44	
    45	"I've a finding on you," she said, to the calf. "I'll not run it again on a hillside. At the ford, when I've a table and I'm not counting days." She tied off the cloth. "You'll keep till then. You've been keeping for years."
    46	
    47	---
    48	
    49	The route came out on the second night, at the fire, because Karis asked for it, for the map.
