    55	She named them in order, as a carter names the fords on a road, and Karis wrote them as she said them, and two of the seven Cael knew. Oxhollow, where they were going. Thornwater, where he had lain in a grain loft with a calf that said eleven. The five between he had never heard of, and he did not ask, and the healer did not describe them; they were names, and then they were ink, and Karis drew a line through the seven that came back round to the first.
    56	
    57	"Twenty days," Oryn said. "Round. Two at a hold, near enough, and the road between, and the road's the part that doesn't move. A hold can wait a day. The road can't be walked faster than it's walked." She had the mule's lead in her hand and was working a burr out of it. "I've walked it round thirty-odd times. I've been late to a hold eleven of them. This is twelve."
    58	
    59	"What do they owe you?" Karis. The research voice; the question meant exactly.
    60	
    61	"Oxhollow shoes the mule and keeps a table I don't have to carry. Thornwater feeds me. One of the five gave me this coat two winters back and won't hear about it." She did not look up from the burr. "And I owe them. There's a hand at Oxhollow I said I'd look at again and haven't. There's a child three stops on that had a fever the day I left, and I don't know. That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."
    62	
    63	"You keep that in a book."
    64	
    65	"No."
    66	
    67	Karis waited, pen up. Oryn got the burr out and dropped it in the fire.
    68	
    69	"I remember who I've had my hands on," she said. "That's the book. A healer who needs a page to know who's owed has stopped looking at them."
    70	
    71	Karis wrote that down too, and Oryn watched her do it and said nothing about it. Seln, across the fire, was listening as he listened to a road.
    72	
    73	"Why," Karis said then. It was the whole question, and she let it be.
    74	
    75	The healer was quiet a moment. Not thinking; this was a woman deciding whether a thing was worth the breath, and deciding it was, once.
    76	
    77	"They certify you," she said, "and they hand you a list. The list is by tier. Bronze and above first, and there's always a Bronze." That was the whole of the city, and she gave it one sentence's room. Then, to the fire, without heat, as a list: "In the city I healed Bronzes with bruises. Out here I set legs. I'm not going back and I'm not stopping. The route is the only thing I own."
    78	
    79	Nobody said anything. Karis wrote it. Lira looked at the healer and then at the fire, and Cael thought — inference, kept as inference — that she had heard the shape of her own sentence in it, the one she had said to a different fire at Lowmarch.
    80	
    81	He catalogued the want because he catalogued wants; it was the first thing he learned about anyone and the last thing he trusted. Seven holds, in order, and a clock. A table at a ford she did not have to carry. A child three stops on. He had been in rooms since he was fourteen where people wanted things and he was somewhere in the wanting — a file to close, a fighter to beat, a phenomenon to write down. She wanted the road walked at the pace it walked. He was not in it anywhere. It was simply true, and it was the first true thing about her he had not had to work for.
    82	
    83	---
    84	
    85	The third day they came down onto the low road.
    86	
    87	It was hoof-cut and plated for a hundred paces where the herd had come off the fan and crossed it — shale on turf, as if the mountain had walked a piece of the way to Oxhollow and thought better of it — and then it was a road again, and empty. Seln read the ground and said twenty-seven, in a voice that meant the carter had been right. Karis lit the fire that night: one point, on the first try, the first thing she had lit since the road, and sat down after it, not hard. Oryn looked at her and said "Eat," and Karis ate.
    88	
    89	The fourth day was river and turf and the valley opening; and Oxhollow, in the afternoon, at the gorge's mouth.
    90	
    91	He read it as he read a board. A river coming out of the mountain grey and fast and going brown as it slowed; a ford where a gravel bar split it, the water over the bar the height of a knee and moving; on the near bank, under the last of the bench, a stockade of grey poles the height of a man and a gate in it standing open; inside, eleven roofs and a yard, carts drawn up nose to tail along the water, mules, the smell of wet rope and dung and something cooking; and against the stockade's inside face, by the gate, a lean-to with a table under it and nothing on the table.
    92	
    93	Nobody sat at that table. He had it before anyone said it: the lean-to was the ford's, and the table was hers, and the place stayed empty twenty days at a time because the one person who used it walked a circuit and the ford had no one else. A city had a healer on every street and a list by tier. Oxhollow had a table.
    94	
    95	There were already people at it. Someone had seen the mule come down the road.
    96	
    97	"You're last," Oryn said to the five of them, at the yard's edge, and it was not unkind; it was the order. "You're the only ones I've had my hands on this month. Sit." And went to the table at the walk she had come down the mountain at, and the line closed round her.
    98	
    99	He saw it from the yard, which was as near as he went; it was hers, not a crew's. An old man with a chest that whistled. A girl with a burn up one arm that had been dressed by somebody who did not know how. A carter with a hand under a cloth — the hand, Cael thought, that she had said she would look at again. She took them in the order they were worst, not the order they had come, and nobody in the line argued the order, which told him how long the table had been hers. She went still over the chest for the seconds and then for the quarter-hour, and the whistle stopped. The girl's arm took the time an apple takes. The carter's hand she unwrapped and looked at for a long while and put both hands on, and said something to the man, low, and he nodded and went; and Cael did not know whether that had been the third until she sat down on the bench behind the table and drank, with the grey in her face, and said, to nobody, "That's three," and the line, which knew what that meant, thinned to the ones who only wanted looking at.
   100	
   101	While she worked, he and Karis took the map to the man who kept the ford.
   102	
   103	A factor's hands, ink and rope; a face that did not change at nine widths, or at the eighth narrowing's drawn break, and changed at the tenth, which was not a width. Karis had drawn the absence as she drew everything, exactly. He read it twice. Then he counted seventy marks onto the table in fives, and said, "Then they go round," and, because Cael had not moved: "That's worth seventy to know before the first cart's in the gorge. I'd sooner pay it for this."
   104	
   105	Cael took the marks. One hundred and sixteen in hand, the smith's six still owed at Lowmarch for a cart that would not be coming back to him. He did not take the log out; the healer's line was thinning, and she had said last, and last had come.
   106	
   107	---
   108	
   109	She read him first, at the table, with the yard going quiet round them as the inn had gone quiet round Teague.
   110	
   111	"Ribs," she said. "Sit there. Don't breathe for me. Breathe how you breathe."
   112	
   113	She put a hand on the tape over the place and a hand on his back behind it, as on the mountain, and went still. Seconds. He counted them. On the bench she had stopped in the middle of them; this time she held to the end, and her face did the thing, smaller — a woman who has heard a thing twice and is confirming the count.
   114	
   115	"Six days," she said. "Those feel like a fortnight. They're knitting. They're knitting as bone knits, only faster than bone does, and there's nothing doing it. No current. Nothing I can feel going along them and telling them to." Her hands came off. "They're doing it themselves. I've had my hands on a hundred ribs. Ribs don't."
   116	
   117	"They always have," he said, because she had asked that on the bench and it was still true.
   118	
   119	"I heard you the first time." She turned. "Leg."
