   122	
   123	Brom gave her the forearm, the raised dark line from wrist to elbow, and she took it in both hands and went still, and this time Cael watched her face instead of the arm, because he wanted the face at the end of a reading that worked. It was the face of a woman reading a page she had read before. "Iron Skin," she said. "Dense. Everything where it goes." She let the arm go. "Ordinary." She said it as the best word she had, and Brom, who had spent years wanting a number, looked at the arm as though ordinary were one, and Cael kept that for the log and did not smile.
   124	
   125	"You again."
   126	
   127	She took his wrist, and put her other hand flat against the side of his neck under the jaw, where — he had learned it from watching four healers, never from being told — a Path was loudest in anyone; where nothing was broken and nothing bound. And went still.
   128	
   129	Longer. He counted to fourteen. Her eyes were on the stockade over his shoulder, and then on nothing, and the face did not do the thing; it had stopped expecting a number. It held, listening, as a woman holds who has put her ear to a wall and is waiting to be sure the room behind it is empty.
   130	
   131	Her hands came off. She sat back on the bench.
   132	
   133	"That's three times," she said. "Three, and the third on a place with nothing wrong in it, so it's not the hurt getting in the way. I wanted to know that." She looked at her hands, and then, deliberately, at him instead. "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. Something's moving. I can't find the banks, so I can't read it, so I can't touch it. That's the rule. I didn't make it."
   134	
   135	The yard was quiet. Karis had the pen up and was not writing, which was rarer than writing.
   136	
   137	"There's two ways to read." She said it to him, but it was the voice she used to a room. "The one I've run on you three times is the short one. Hands on the hurt, a count of ten, and I get the hurt and what's under it — Iron Skin, Ember, Wind. Every healer runs it. It costs nothing worth counting; it's the one you run on a road with a line waiting. The long one's the other thing. Both hands, and all of me, run through all of you, slow — not looking for a hurt, looking at how you're built. It takes minutes, not seconds. You have to be still, and you have to be whole, because a hurt shouts and I can't hear the rest over it. And it costs what a mending costs. I don't run it on a road." She turned the skin over in her hands and did not drink. "I've never run it on you. You've never once been still and whole in front of me. I met you on your back with three ribs gone."
   138	
   139	"And if I were."
   140	
   141	"When you're not broken, I run the long one. It won't read you either. I want to feel it not read you properly." She said it as she had said ordinary — a finding she was willing to be wrong about and did not expect to be.
   142	
   143	He did not answer that. There was nothing in it that asked to be answered; it was a woman telling a patient what the next visit was.
   144	
   145	"Three things," she said, "and then I've a mule to load. When you heal — when you've done it on your own, the way you say you always have — does it hurt in the right places?"
   146	
   147	He had never been asked. Four healers, three assessors, a panel, and Coss with a file, and none of them had asked whether it hurt in the right places. He answered it honestly, because the only strategy this woman would not see straight through was the truth. "Yes."
   148	
   149	"Faster than other people, or slower."
   150	
   151	"Faster."
   152	
   153	"Has any healer ever got anything? Under. Not the ribs. You."
   154	
   155	"No."
   156	
   157	She nodded, once, the nod of a count coming out as expected. She did not write it down. Karis's pen was still up; the healer did not look at it, and Cael understood — it arrived as findings arrived, whole — that she was not going to. Karis kept what she learned on a page so that it would outlast her. This woman kept it where she kept the seven holds and the carter's hand and the child three stops on, and nowhere else. She would remember his ribs at Thornwater in nine days as she remembered the coat. That was the book.
   158	
   159	"That's the visit," Oryn said, and stood up, which took her one try, and went to the mule.
   160	
   161	---
   162	
   163	She loaded it herself, at the gate, with the sun going off the bench above the roofs. The roll under the strap; the panniers, which had carried a box for four days and now carried the kit. Lira, on two feet with a hand on the stockade, watched her and said nothing; Brom held the mule's lead while she tightened a girth and gave it back without being asked, and she said "Hm," to him, and it was thanks. It was not a leaving that had been arranged. It was a healer at the end of a stop.
   164	
   165	"What do we owe," Cael said. He had the marks in the satchel, and he had priced a leg on the board and did not know what it was worth at a table.
   166	
   167	"Oxhollow feeds the mule. That's Oxhollow's." She took the lead, and looked at him, and it was the look from the third reading, the one with no number in it and no awe. "Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
   168	
   169	He had a sentence. He had had it since the second night's fire, and it was a good sentence, and it asked her to walk south with five people instead of north with a mule, and he did not say it. He catalogued not saying it; that was the whole of what he did with it. She had seven holds and a child she did not know about. He had a board. She had told him at a fire what she owned, and it was not a thing you asked a person to set down for a crew that had known her four days.
   170	
   171	"Nine days," he said.
   172	
   173	She went out through the gate and up the north road at the carter's walk, and did not look back, because looking back was not a thing the walk had in it.
   174	
   175	---
   176	
   177	Teague's crew came in off the east road at dusk.
   178	
   179	He heard the cart before he saw it, and the first thing he catalogued was that it was a cart. Two wheels, a bed, a load under canvas, and it was not in a gorge. Four with it. The tall woman walked ahead with the spear across her shoulders and her hands hung over it. The broad man with the crossbow and the limp had the cart's tail. The young one was between the shafts, pulling, and pulling it well. And Teague came last, as he had come last through Lowmarch's door, and stopped at the yard's edge where the water began and did the thing Cael had watched him do once: he looked at the woman, and at the man with the limp, and at the young one at the shafts, each for the length of a breath, and only when he had them did he look at the yard, and the yard had five people in it he had read across a room thirty-five days ago and not spoken to.
   180	
   181	He had heard, then. The ford was on Pike's page with his hold struck through and their five names under it, and Pike read the page aloud; the traverse had come down the mountain ahead of them on a carter's tongue. Pike had said it, at the plank, asked by nobody: he would speak to them when they had cost him something. The ford had been his slip.
   182	
   183	Teague crossed the yard at the pace of a man going to a table he knew. He stopped a stride and a half off, which was Oryn's distance, and Cael marked the coincidence and did not trust it, and the man looked at him — at him, not at the tape under his shirt or the calf he was standing wrong on, though he had seen both from the water — and spoke.
   184	
   185	"You cleared Thornwater's ford with five and lost a cart on the high bench with five."
   186	
   187	"Yes."
   188	
   189	"The ford was good work. The bench was a cart."
   190	
   191	He took it apart, because that was what he did with a sentence that had been aimed. It was not unkind; he listened for unkind and it was not in it anywhere. It was a claim, with the evidence in it, and a ruling: two jobs, five people on each; one done well; one that had cost a cart. Every word was true, and the ruling was the one he would have written himself over the traverse if he had been Pike, or Vell, or a boy in a boarding house with a notebook and a circuit to price. He had been on the far side of this a thousand times. He had never once been on the near side of it from a man who did it as he did it — no seal, no bracket, no tier; a ledger in a head and a sentence that said what the ledger said. He had no argument. The bench had been a cart.
   192	
   193	Then, the rest of it, in the same voice: "The board's going to have you and me on the same contract inside a season. Be good enough that I'm glad."
   194	
   195	He did not wait to be answered. It had not been a question. He went back across the yard at the same pace and said something short to the tall woman, and the young one let the shafts down, and the man with the limp hung the crossbow on the cart, and the four of them stood at the water and let the mule drink and did not look at the five of them again, because the ledger had been read and there was nothing else on the page.
   196	
   197	Cael sat down on the bank, with the tape telling his ribs where the edge was, and took the log out.
   198	
   199	*Eighty-fourth day. Oxhollow. The map's paid; a hundred and sixteen in hand, six owed for a cart. Lira's on two feet. The healer's gone north.*
   200	
   201	*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
   202	
   203	He closed it and did not write anything else, because the rest of what he had was not a sentence yet.
   204	
   205	Across the yard the young one picked the shafts up. The tall woman went into the water first, with the spear across her shoulders, and the cart went in after her with the broad man at its tail, and Teague last; and the four of them walked Oxhollow's ford at dusk with the river to their knees and the cart's wheels finding the bar, and nobody spoke, and nobody splashed, and the crossbow hung where the man could reach it without looking. They walked it the way carters walked the void roads. Quiet. A crew that had walked this a hundred times and had never once lost the count of itself in the water. They came up onto the far bank and the cart came up after them, streaming, and the south road took them, and the light went off the river.
   206	
   207	He turned on the bank. Behind him, above the roofs, where the north road climbed along under the bench, there was one more thing in the last of the light, and he found it because he was looking. A mile up, by the eye. A mule, and a woman walking on the down-slope side of it at a pace that had not changed since the gate, small against the bench, and then smaller, going the way the route went.

