    54	
    55	There were fourteen slips. He gave each the same attention, the bark and the hide and the two shingles, and he sorted them as he read, as he had once sorted a board in Ardenmere by what each card would cost him. There was a fence for somebody on the north water, at five marks. A string of mules wanted walking to the turf fire, westward, and that one he passed over without stopping. A carter on the south road wanted a wheel fetched. There was hay to be got in at the upper inn, for a mark and the hay-loft to sleep in.
    56	
    57	When he had done, the paper with the horned head was at the top of the heap in his mind, and it had got there so quickly that he distrusted it. He made himself say why, in items, as he would have made a stranger say it.
    58	
