   113	It was the neatest thing Cael had ever seen him think of. He stood in a loop that went round his chest and under both arms, facing downhill, with his hands behind his back; and when Karis, up at the log, raised her arm, he walked down the mountain. Seln and Cael went down with him, hauling hand over hand. For every yard that three people descended the cart rose one, nose first, half buried, with the plates parting round her and closing behind as water closes behind a punt.
   114	
   115	"Hold," said Karis, in the low carrying voice that she kept for a reading-room.
   116	
   117	They held. Lira scrambled across below the cart and wedged slabs under both wheels, and the rope was carried up to the next island, and the log after it.
   118	
   119	The first stage went as he had planned it, and the second did not.
   120	
   121	Along the gorge-ward edge of the fan there ran a gully, a chute as wide as a road, where the loosest of the stone went down toward the drop; and the whole skin of the slope leaned that way, like a floor in an old house. Twenty yards into the second stage the cart began to lean with it. She did not slide back. She went sideways, quite gently, a foot for every yard she climbed, floating off the line of the rope toward the chute; and up at the island the peeled log began to creep along its notch after her.
