   127	She was there already. They had knotted the box-lashings into a second line, thirty yards of it, and made one end fast to the cart's near side. Lira had carried the other up the slope and to the left, away from the chute, to a boulder as big as a barrel. She sat down behind it now with both boots against the rock. She passed the line round her back, under her arms, and took it in her two fists in front of her.
   128	
   129	"It's a thing I can do sitting," she said. "Walk."
   130	
   131	They walked. The line came taut across the slope and the cart's head swung, a little, back toward the island.
   132	
   133	Then the fan under the near wheel let go.
   134	
   135	It was a small slip, a barrowful of plates running out from under the rim, and the cart dropped a foot and snatched. Cael saw the line take Lira. It lifted her off her seat and dragged her a yard up the boulder's face, with her heels scraping, and he saw what happened in her calves, because he had been watching for it for sixty days. Every sinew in her stood ready to be somewhere else. It was the burst, asking, with the whole of her Path behind it.
   136	
   137	In his own feet Wind leaned with hers, as it always did.
   138	
   139	She sat down. She did it on purpose, hard, as one sits on a lid, and drove both boots against the rock again, and the line burned through her fists for a hand's breadth and stopped. The cart's nose came round.
   140	
   141	"Walk," she said, through her teeth.
   142	
   143	He walked. Nine fragments had come up at the snatch, and he went on down the slope with the rope in his hands, putting them down one at a time. Compression had felt the jerk come along the hemp and had offered to take it through his frame into the hill. Anchor had been asking about the island since first light. *There?* A point fixed against that notch, and told to stay, would have been a better thing than any log; it would not have walked, or worn, or needed a man's weight to hold it. He owned the craft of it. He told it that the log was Brom's, and to leave it.
   144	
   145	The Iron read was harder to refuse, because it offered the thing he was being paid to know.
   146	
   147	It could have told him the weight of that slope. Within a pace and a half of his boots it would have given him every plate, and which way the whole loose skin meant to go, a breath before it went; he would have felt the slip under the near wheel coming, as he felt a punch. He was a surveyor today, by the terms of a slip, and he carried an instrument that no surveyor west of the Line could have bought, and he kept it shut.
   148	
   149	He turned that over through two more stages, since his hands were busy and his head was not.
