   250	
   251	There was ice on the kettle in the morning, a skin as thin as a leaf, the first that any of them had seen past the Line. Lira ran the ninth hundred with her breath smoking. He paced it, and wrote, and they drew the cart on with Brom leaning into his rope on the up-slope side, where the weight of him kept her from drifting toward the edge. Wherever a gully came down the face it had laid a tongue of loose plates right across the shelf, and on those she floated, as he had said she would. He walked against the strap there with his whole body slanted, like a man in a gale.
   252	
   253	The tenth was slab, and the eleventh was slab with a crack.
   254	
   255	Lira went out for the twelfth, and she did not come back at a run.
   256	
