   258	
   259	She did not speak when she reached them. She took Cael by the sleeve and drew him three steps out from under the buttress, and pointed with her chin, up and to the west.
   260	
   261	He looked where she showed him, and saw the mountain.
   262	
   263	A quarter of a mile along, and far up the slope, another shelf ran across the face. It was narrower than theirs. Along it, for the length of a long field, the grey plates of the hillside lay in a row, humped and overlapping, each heap about the size of the cart; and there was a gap of bare rock between each heap and the next, and all the gaps were the same.
   264	
   265	He counted the heaps, since he could no more have helped it than breathing, and he had reached twenty-two when one of them lifted its head.

