     3	There were thirty of them, and he counted twice to be sure.
     4	
     5	Twenty-six were full grown. Four were half the size, with plates of a paler grey that had not yet gathered any green, and those four kept to the inside of the shelf, against the hill. Each of the grown animals was as long as the cart and a good deal broader. It stood low on four legs like the stumps of trees, and its back and both flanks were roofed over with horn, in slabs that overlapped as slates overlap on a house, the largest along the spine and each with its keel. Lichen grew on the old ones in rings. When one of them stood still, which was nearly always, it was a heap of the mountain's own stone, and his eye slid off it.
     6	
     7	The head was small for such a body, and carried at the height of a man's knee. It ended in something like the beak of a tortoise, and with that the animal scraped the rock. He could hear it, now that he knew to listen: a dry patient rasping, thirty times over, like a yard full of masons dressing stone a long way off. Under that there was another sound, whenever one of them shifted its weight, for the plates moved on each other with a low clatter, as a slate roof settles when the sun goes off it.
     8	
