    32	
    33	He paced, and wrote. Karis laid her brass on the rock and bent over her thread, and came to him with each figure as a clerk brings a paper to a desk. Brom leaned into his rope on the up-slope side, with his eyes on the cart's near wheel and never once on the hill, like a man passing a dog that he has been told will bite if it is stared at. The dry hub ticked at every turn, and up on the face the rasping went on without a break. Each time he raised his eyes from the page the grey heaps were where they had been.
    34	
