    44	
    45	Karis stood looking at the ring for some time.
    46	
    47	"I want a fire," she said.
    48	
    49	Lira turned round. Brom had been lowering the tail-board with both hands under it, and he stopped with it halfway.
    50	
    51	"Hear me first. The country's rule has three things in it, and Pike's *loud* has the same three, since it's the same rule. For the hounds we found out which one mattered, and it cost four of us a month. I don't know which matters to those." She tipped her head at the roof, and at what was above it. "I shall have to find out, because the last column of his table is mine, and a carter who comes over this bench with a Force at his tail-board ought to be told what he may do with him. Of the three, there is one that I can test this evening and then take back."
    52	
    53	"Fire," said Cael.
    54	
    55	"It's struck with a flint. It will be the size of my two hands, under a roof of rock, at three hundred and fifty yards; and if one head comes up, Seln puts his coat over it, and we've lost a coat." She folded her arms, carefully, on account of her palms. "A Path I can't take back, and a shout I'd sooner not try on purpose. But I should like to cross one of the three off tonight, while it's cheap."
    56	
    57	"And if it isn't cheap?" said Brom. "If it's the one?"
    58	
    59	"Then we learn it with a fire that a coat can put out, and not next week with one we need."
