    61	Cael had the ruling, and he did not hurry it. He had lifted the rule from tinderboxes once before, above a wash, on the strength of a page that had been tested at a ford; and this was tested by nobody. He turned her reasoning over for the place where it would break, as she had once asked four people to do in a loft, and what he found was that she had already chosen the one test whose failure they could best afford.
    62	
    63	"Hands," he said.
    64	
    65	There were five. Brom's came up last, and he set the tail-board down on its chains first, so gently that no link spoke.
    66	
    67	Seln struck the flint, and Lira lay on her stomach at the mouth of the overhang, where the roof stopped, and watched the upper shelf with her chin on her fists, since her eyes were the best of the five. The tinder caught. A thread of smoke went up and flattened under the rock and crept out along it into the dusk, and when the first twigs had taken there was light on all their faces.
    68	
    69	"Well?" said Karis. She had Hesk's clock open on her knee.
    70	
    71	"Nothing." Lira kept her head where it was. "They're scraping. The third from this end has lain down; it folded up its front legs first, like a cow."
    72	
    73	"Did it look this way?"
    74	
    75	"It looked at its own feet."
    76	
    77	They gave it a quarter of an hour by the clock, and then another, with the fire built up to the size of a hat, and thirty beaks rasped on in the dark overhead. Karis shut the clock.
    78	
    79	"One," she said, and wrote it. "Fire: lit and fed for half an hour, in plain sight, at three hundred and fifty yards, and not a head." She looked up. "I shan't call it struck off for good. I call it struck off at that distance, and at that size. But I'm a good deal warmer than I was."
    80	
    81	"Twenty-two years," said Brom, to the flames, in a fair imitation of a carter, and Lira laughed into her sleeve.
    82	
    83	They ate hot food for the first time since the wash. Afterward Karis sat with her notebook shut on her finger, looking at the black line of the upper shelf, and Cael knew what she was going to ask before she drew the breath for it.
    84	
    85	"I want tomorrow, all of it. We do no hundreds, and I sit on a rock in front of this place with the clock and look at them from first light until I can't see." She turned to him. "It will cost a day's lines, and I know what that is in flour."
    86	
    87	"It isn't a day off the map," he said. "It's the map. The column says whether it takes a cart, and why, and those are part of why."
    88	
    89	"That's what I'd have argued."
    90	
    91	"Then I've saved you a sentence."
    92	
    93	"I had four ready." She opened the notebook, and her eyes had the brightness in them that he had seen over a charcoal river. "I shall want a second pair of eyes, since I can't watch both ends of a line three hundred yards long; and a hand, for when mine stops."
    94	
