    96	
    97	She took her seat at first light, on a slab ten yards out from the overhang's mouth, with her knees drawn up and the clock beside her on a folded stocking.
    98	
    99	Cael sat on her left with the notebook, and Lira lay on the rock at her right, on her stomach again. Brom stayed under the roof with the cart and the boxes, mending a strap, and Seln had gone on westward before any of them woke. There was ice on the slab. The sun had not yet reached into the gorge, and thirty heaps of grey stone lay along the upper shelf with frost on their backs.
   100	
   101	"The east end is mine," said Karis, "and you have the west. Lira takes whichever of them does something. Nobody is to tell me what he thinks it means; I want what it did, and when."
   102	
   103	The first thing she had from them was the spacing, and she had it within the hour.
   104	
   105	"Count the gaps, in lengths. I've no means of taping them from here."
   106	
   107	He counted. Between the westernmost animal and the next there was room for one of them and half another, and between that one and the third it was the same, and so along the line as far as he could see it clearly. Where a half-grown calf stood inside its dam the two of them made one place, and the gap was kept from the pair.
   108	
   109	"A length and a half," he said. "Everywhere."
   110	
   111	"I make it the same. Watch the sixth from your end; it's about to come forward, for it's scraped out what's in front of it."
   112	
   113	The sixth moved up a pace. It did so with great deliberation, one thick leg at a time, and a breath afterward the seventh did the same, and the eighth after that. He watched the gap close and open again down eleven animals, like a pulse going along a rope, and when it had passed, every gap was a length and a half once more.
   114	
   115	"They don't look at each other to do that," said Lira. "Their heads are down, and they can't see past their own beaks."
   116	
   117	"Write that she said so," said Karis, "and the time."
   118	
   119	The second thing took her until the sun was on the shelf. High on the face, a little west of the leading animal, a vein of white stone ran down the slope as broad as a door, and at the first hour by the clock the leader's beak had been a cart's length short of it. She had him enter that. At the second hour the white vein was behind the leader's tail, and the whole line, thirty animals and all their gaps, lay forty yards farther along the mountain than it had.
   120	
   121	"Did you see one of them walk?" she said.
   122	
   123	He thought about it honestly.
   124	
   125	"I saw the sixth come up a pace, and the ones behind it, and twice more I saw a leg lift. I never saw the line go anywhere."
   126	
   127	"Nor I, and I watched one animal for a count of three hundred without blinking more than I could help. It took four steps in that time, each of them while it was scraping." She rubbed her eyes with the back of her wrist. "They move as the hand of this clock moves. One looks away, and it's later."
